# Checkwright's gate-binary target roster — gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Consumer payload.
# One Rust target triple per line; `#`-comments and blank lines are stripped,
# the same line grammar scripts/gates.list uses. This file is the single surface
# asserting which platforms a released gate binary is built for: the publish
# workflow derives its build matrix from it, and the installer reads the payload
# copy of it to tell "this platform was never committed to" (omit and declare)
# from "committed to, artifact missing" (a broken payload).
#
# WHAT LICENSES A LINE HERE, stated mechanically so the session that observes
# the green executes rather than re-derives. A target <T> joins when ONE run,
# at or after this paragraph's landing commit, carries BOTH of:
#
#   1. `native-artifacts` green for <T> in .github/workflows/gates.yml — its
#      binary and .sha256 sidecar produced by the release's own build command
#      and uploaded; and
#   2. a platform install-smoke leg green having CONSUMED that upload —
#      INSTALLER_SMOKE_ARTIFACTS_DIR pointed at the downloaded directory — and
#      having reached the artifact-present branch, whose completion assertion
#      installer/consumer-smoke/run-smoke.sh owns and emits.
#
# That is *produced and exercised* with no host-built stand-in anywhere in it.
# On that observation, three edits and nothing else: this file gains <T>'s line,
# native/runners.list already names its runner, and docs/install.md
# §Requirements' platform declaration flips <T> from `held` to `joined`. Those
# three move together or not at all.
#
# ONE OBLIGATION THE FIRST OF THOSE THREE INCURS. The binary's source stamp is
# git's content identity for EVERY tracked file under the crate root
# (check-gate-binary-fresh, via git ls-files), and this file is one. So a line
# added here reds that gate with no Rust touched, and the same commit owes
# `bash gate-sdk/bin/build-native.sh`. native/runners.list is in the same set.
#
# **Ruled by the OPERATOR 2026-09-07**, through the lead's question relay in a
# lead session and lead-relayed. The alternative — reading one green macOS
# install-smoke leg as sufficient — was put and refused, on the ground that the
# leg's own header pre-refuses exactly that reading. A later session finding the
# textual case persuasive is reading a settled question.
#
# THE OWNER OF THE WIDENING IS THE PREDICATE ABOVE, not a queue entry. An entry
# is a lifetime that ends; pointing a live precondition at one is what left this
# file naming `platform-support-ci-matrix` for weeks after that entry retired.
#
# Every line here is evidence rather than capability, and the roster's size
# argues nothing either way. The two are easy to conflate here, and conflating
# them is what makes a roster widen on a plan instead of on a run:
#   - A line means a discharged precondition and nothing weaker: the run above
#     is what §Consumer payload's join bound asks for, and a triple without one
#     stays off this file however reachable it looks. x86_64-apple-darwin is the
#     standing case — its producer half is green on every run and its consumer
#     leg now exists — and it is still not a line here, because no single run
#     has yet carried both halves.
#   - Runner availability is not the constraint. This repository is public, so
#     macOS and arm64-Linux hosted runners are already available to it; what is
#     missing is a run bought on one, not a machine to buy it on. Measured
#     2026-08-25. A header claiming the workflows have no runner for a platform
#     would be reading a fact about this repo as a fact about the world.
#   - Cross-compiling a target from the Linux runner is refused for the reason
#     the bound gives rather than on cost: it would publish an artifact no run
#     has ever executed. `native-artifacts` therefore builds each target on that
#     target's own platform (native/runners.list) and never with `--target`
#     pointed off the host.
#   - WSL is Linux and resolves to this same target, so docs/install.md
#     §Requirements' Windows-through-WSL claim is served exactly as far as that
#     page already claims and no further. Native Windows is no longer
#     *impossible*: the crate now compiles for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, held
#     there by the cross-check step in .github/workflows/gates.yml's `gates`
#     job. Compiling is not the join bound, and neither is the run above on its
#     own: that triple fails the FIRST of §Consumer payload's two bounds, since
#     docs/install.md §Requirements states Windows as running through WSL and
#     not natively, so the platform declaration there does not carry it at all.
#     An iteration named *unblock* removes a blocker; it does not grant a
#     permission, and a reader taking the one for the other widens this roster
#     on a plan.
#
# This is not a narrowing of the trajectory — the objectives are the direction
# and this roster is the tree. What keeps the omit-and-declare path the normal
# path — rather than the dead code the vacuous-green class exists to refuse —
# is the HELD REMAINDER and not the roster's size: Intel macOS is still held,
# native Windows is not declared at all, and every host outside docs/install.md
# §Requirements' declaration block still meets that path on day one. A target
# joins only on the observation above, and never beyond what that section
# already states.
#
# What a second line costs, now that one has landed and the figure is paid
# rather than estimated: one runner mapping in native/runners.list; the consumer
# smoke, which never blocked on it, because the smoke steers itself at its own
# host by default and takes a producer's artifact directory through
# INSTALLER_SMOKE_ARTIFACTS_DIR when a caller hands it one — the re-entry this
# file used to record as unbuilt, and it is built; and the item this accounting
# did not carry until the RELEASE path was read against it. A second line puts a
# second leg into .github/workflows/publish.yml's roster-derived build matrix,
# and that leg needed a platform floor and a resolved hasher the first one never
# did. Both now live in scripts/ci-macos-floor.sh and scripts/ci-build-artifact.sh,
# called by the release leg and the CI producer alike, so a further line on a
# platform class those two already bootstrap costs a runner mapping again — and
# one on a class they do not costs its own floor first.
# gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Consumer payload owns both halves;
# installer/README.md §The consumer smoke owns the smoke's own side of it.
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
aarch64-apple-darwin
